---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { url, unitUrl } from '../lib/url';
import { renderMath } from '../lib/math';
import { units } from '../data/units';
import { syllabus, SYLLABUS_PDF, SYLLABUS_PAGE } from '../data/syllabus';

const unitOf = (n: number) => units.find((u) => u.number === n)!;

const constants = [
  { name: 'acceleration of free fall near the Earth', value: '$g \\approx 9.8\\,\\text{m/s}^2$ (so $9.8\\,\\text{N/kg}$)', where: 'syllabus 1.2' },
  { name: 'speed of light (and all electromagnetic waves) in a vacuum', value: '$c = 3.0 \\times 10^8\\,\\text{m/s}$', where: 'syllabus 3.3' },
  { name: 'one light-year', value: '$9.5 \\times 10^{15}\\,\\text{m}$', where: 'syllabus 6.2.2' },
  { name: 'Hubble constant (current estimate)', value: '$H_0 = 2.2 \\times 10^{-18}\\,\\text{s}^{-1}$', where: 'syllabus 6.2.3' },
];

const ourValues = [
  { name: 'one year in seconds', value: '$365 \\times 24 \\times 3600 \\approx 3.15 \\times 10^7\\,\\text{s}$', why: 'calculated' },
  { name: 'density of water', value: '$1000\\,\\text{kg/m}^3$ ($1.0\\,\\text{g/cm}^3$)', why: 'standard value' },
  { name: 'density of sea water', value: '$1030\\,\\text{kg/m}^3$', why: 'typical value (it changes a little with salt and temperature)' },
];
---

<Base title="Syllabus and sources" description="The Cambridge syllabus that Clear Physics follows, and the sources used for data.">
  <p class="kicker">Cambridge IGCSE Physics 0625 · syllabus 2026–2028</p>
  <h1>Syllabus and sources</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    The lessons follow the official Cambridge syllabus.
    This page links to the syllabus, shows how the course follows it, and lists the other sources used for data.
  </p>

  <h2 id="syllabus">1. The syllabus</h2>
  <div class="card doc">
    <p class="doc-title"><strong>Cambridge IGCSE Physics 0625 — Syllabus for exams in 2026, 2027 and 2028</strong></p>
    <p class="muted small">Cambridge Assessment International Education · Version 2, published December 2025 (no changes that affect teaching).</p>
    <p class="links">
      <a href={SYLLABUS_PDF}>Read the syllabus (PDF)</a>
      <a href={SYLLABUS_PAGE}>Official 0625 page (past papers, updates)</a>
    </p>
  </div>
  <p>
    We do not copy the text of the syllabus. The lessons explain each learning point in our own words.
    For the exact wording, always read the official document.
  </p>
  <p class="muted small">
    Cambridge has also published a new syllabus for exams from 2029. This site follows the 2026–2028 syllabus.
  </p>

  <h2 id="core-extended">2. Core and Extended</h2>
  <p>
    Each topic of the syllabus has two columns of learning points:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Core</strong>: for all students.</li>
    <li><strong>Supplement</strong>: extra content for students who take the <strong>Extended</strong> papers (Core + Supplement).</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Paper</th><th>Core students</th><th>Extended students</th><th>Weight</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Multiple choice (45 min, 40 questions)</td><td>Paper 1</td><td>Paper 2</td><td>30%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Theory (1 h 15 min, 80 marks)</td><td>Paper 3</td><td>Paper 4</td><td>50%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Practical test (Paper 5) or written alternative (Paper 6)</td><td colspan="2">Paper 5 or Paper 6</td><td>20%</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="muted small">Core students can get grades C to G. Extended students can get grades A* to G. Source: syllabus, section 2.</p>

  <h3>How we write the codes</h3>
  <p>
    Each lesson shows the parts of the syllabus it teaches. A code like <code>1.8 C2</code> means
    <em>topic 1.8, Core learning point 2</em>. A code like <code>1.8 S4</code> means
    <em>topic 1.8, Supplement learning point 4</em>.
    Lessons with Supplement content show the label <span class="tag ext">Extended</span>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="map">3. From the syllabus to the course</h2>
  <p>
    The syllabus has 6 sections. The course teaches them in {units.length} units over two years.
    The order is a little different from the syllabus: Space physics comes last, because it uses ideas from many other units.
  </p>

  {syllabus.map((section) => (
    <section class="section">
      <h3>{section.number}. {section.title}</h3>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="map">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Syllabus topic</th><th>Clear Physics unit</th><th>Lessons</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {section.topics.map((t) => {
              const u = unitOf(t.unit);
              return (
                <tr>
                  <td>
                    <strong>{t.code}</strong> {t.title}
                    {t.parts && <ul class="parts">{t.parts.map((p) => <li>{p}</li>)}</ul>}
                  </td>
                  <td>
                    <a href={unitUrl(u.number)}>Unit {u.number}: {u.title}</a>
                  </td>
                  <td class="num">{u.lessonCount}</td>
                </tr>
              );
            })}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>
  ))}

  <h2 id="language">4. Words, symbols and units</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Command words</strong> (<em>state, describe, explain, calculate…</em>): the syllabus gives a list of command words used in exam questions.
      We explain each one in simple English on the <a href={url('key-words/#command-words')}>key words page</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Symbols and units</strong>: the lessons use the symbols and units listed in the syllabus
      (section 4, “Symbols and units for physical quantities”), for example <em>p</em> for pressure and Pa for pascal.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Equations</strong>: we write equations in the form used in the syllabus, and we change the subject with algebra (no formula triangles). The full list is on the <a href={url('equations/')}>Equations to know</a> page.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="values">5. Values used in the lessons</h2>
  <h3>Values from the syllabus</h3>
  <p>Students must know these values. We use them in all lessons and practice questions.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Value</th><th>Where</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {constants.map((c) => (
          <tr><td>{c.name}</td><td set:html={renderMath(c.value)} /><td class="muted">{c.where}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3>Other values</h3>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Value</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {ourValues.map((c) => (
          <tr><td>{c.name}</td><td set:html={renderMath(c.value)} /><td class="muted">{c.why}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="data">6. Data sources</h2>
  <ul class="sources">
    <li>
      <strong>Planets</strong> (Unit 2, Unit 17): distance from the Sun, orbital period and speed, mass, diameter, density, surface gravity, temperature and number of moons.
      Source: <a href="https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/">NASA Planetary Fact Sheet</a> (checked October 2026). Values are rounded.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Densities of materials</strong> (Unit 2, Unit 6): typical values at room temperature from standard reference tables, rounded.
      Real samples can be a little different (wood and oil especially).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Specific heat capacities</strong> (Unit 8): rounded values from data tables
      (water 4200, aluminium 900, iron 450, copper 385, lead 130 J/(kg °C)), for example the
      <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Table_of_specific_heat_capacities">table of specific heat capacities</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Refractive indices</strong> (Unit 10): typical values for visible light (water 1.33, Perspex 1.49, glass about 1.5, diamond 2.42).
      Real values change a little with the type of glass and the colour of the light.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Mains supply</strong> (Units 13–14): 230 V in many countries; 220 V, 50 Hz in Thailand
      (<a href="https://www.worldstandards.eu/electricity/plug-voltage-by-country/thailand/">World Standards: Thailand</a>).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Electricity in Thailand</strong> (Unit 5, energy resources): natural gas generates more than half of the country’s electricity.
      Sources: <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Energy_in_Thailand">Energy in Thailand</a> (Wikipedia, citing the International Energy Agency)
      and <a href="https://ember-energy.org/countries-and-regions/thailand/">Ember: Thailand</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Everyday numbers</strong> (speeds of cars, masses of people, depths of pools…): realistic round values chosen by us, so that the answers make sense.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="ours">7. What we write ourselves</h2>
  <p>
    The explanations, examples, figures, simulations, practice questions and tests are our own work.
    They are written for students who learn physics in English as a second language.
    See <a href={url('teachers/')}>For teachers</a> for how the lessons are written.
  </p>
  <p class="card small">
    Clear Physics is an independent resource. It is not affiliated with or endorsed by Cambridge University Press &amp; Assessment.
  </p>
</Base>

<style>
  .kicker { margin: 0; font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .lead { font-size: 1.1rem; }
  .doc p { margin: 0.25rem 0; }
  .doc-title { font-size: 1.05rem; }
  .links { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.5rem 1.5rem; margin-top: 0.6rem !important; }
  .links a { font-weight: 600; }
  .section h3 { margin-top: 1.6rem; }
  .map td { vertical-align: top; }
  .num { white-space: nowrap; }
  .parts { margin: 0.3rem 0 0; padding-left: 1.1rem; font-size: 0.88rem; color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .parts li { margin: 0.1rem 0; }
  .sources li { margin-bottom: 0.7rem; }
  code { font-size: 0.9em; }
</style>
